ep sequencing we also confirmed  that there were deletions in  up to six to seven percent of um  the genomic dna analyzed so the the  deletion pattern  was mostly below 50 base pairs in  deletion but we did have some  larger dna deletion of about 130 base  pairs  kind of indicating that both uh that  that exon  45 was removed from this reporter system  so with that we were able to show  um not only in vitro delivery but we're  also able to show  in vivo delivery into this reporter  mouse  so we we tested various cell lines um  and we found efficient delivery in in  all of the cell lines indicated  we also tested um multiple endogenous  loci and found efficient editing  so once we established that nanomedic  worked  we went we wanted to develop  a culture system uh that was suspension  based for more scalable production of  nanomedic  and i'll go into the development of how  how we did that using electroporation  so our  previous system used adherent cells  adherent 293 t  cells we also use fetal bovine serum  for the media and a lipid transfection  reagent  the problem i had when i was trying to  scale up the  nanomedic production the sedharensa line  is that the adherent cells  they came off the plates really easily  it was hard to get even  seating density when i went to larger  plates uh we wanted to remove the fetal  bovine serum to make it xeno-free to  make it applicable for  for more potentially uh clinical  application later on  and the the lipid transfection reagent  um  there wasn't much room for optimization  of delivery of your dna using this  so we um we found that  just technically it was difficult using  this adherent system  so switching to a suspension  hex system available from thermo fissure  that can be grown at high cell density  we  also switched to a completely different  um  chemically defined xenofree media which  could be  gmp compatible and we also wanted to  move away from  lipid transfection to electroporation  using the mac site platform which is um  shown in in the bottom right machine so  we we wanted to hopefully achieve  scalable  um production of nanomedic which  was easier technically to handle and was  also applicable for for clinical  application so  i mean uh the pictures on the bottom  show okay if i was generating an  inherent  um anonymity from adhering cells i'd  have a lot of plates  like indicated in the bottom left uh the  shaker flask i could  minimize the the space taken the tissue  culture hood and  and also reduce the handling of these  cells so that's what we were aiming for  in the beginning  and just the electroporation protocol we  used was  we expanded our hex cells and and  patches  passage them the day before  electroporation on the day of  electroporation we exchanged in mac site  buffer which is just a salt solution and  a universal buffer from  for all cell types we then mix the cells  with  our plasmid dna for for nanomedic  added them to the cuvette and  electroporated  after electroporation we rested the  cells for 30 to 40 minutes at 37 degrees  to allow for  membrane recovery and then we culture  the cells as normal for 36 to 48 hours  and harvested the nanomedic particles we  would then  inoculate them on reporter cells and  check the flow  the gfp expression by flow cytometry  the the initial experiment we did  however was um we checked  gfp dna plasmid expression and we wanted  to compare if electroporation was better  than lipid transfection  uh in the suspension cells so what you  can see  here is just gfp expression um this is a  histogram plot  using two different max site energies so  it's a heck protocol and optimization  for protocol these are  two somewhat lower energy protocols  available on the electroporation  platform  and we compare that with the lipid  transfection  reagent that came with the lv max cells  so what you can see is with the the max  8 system you can get nearly 100 percent  of the cells expressing gfp and the main  fluorescence intensity was  was shifted um far to the right and i  mean these cells were really green after  48 hours  visually by eye with the lipid  transfection region you can also get  high efficiency of gfb plasmid delivery  however the the expression intensity was  was a lot lower than electroporation  and not only that if you look at the  viability the viability of the cells was  around 80 after 48 hours with  electroporation  versus 40 with the lipid transfection  region so  we found better delivery efficiency and  higher viability  um using the maxsite platform  so next just to kind of um  to summarize the the process again we  electroporate our  nanometic plasmids um into  the suspension hexels by the maxi  instrument  um we would harvest our nanometer  particles and then  inoculate them onto reporter cells and  then if we get gfp expression  that indicates that nanomedic was  efficiently delivered into the reporter  cells and  active so this  slide shows the optimization of the  energy used  for um electroporating the producer cell  to generate nanomedic  uh and so what happened was if you go  from low energy indicated by 4  to the highest energy indicated by 10  here  increasing the energy increased the  amount of functional  nonometic particles from the producer  cells which  is indicated by the gfp expression  and the reporter cells so the higher  energy  the more nanomedic produced from from  these cells  energy 9 and 10 had comparable output of  nanomedic  but the cell viability was a bit better  in nine  uh one one kind of challenge we  encountered when we were  optimizing was that um  production of of uh  resulted in a lower cell number and  lower cell viability  um after 36 hours and so  one way we were able to kind of resolve  this issue was to add  dna so we would electroporate the cells  and after electroporating the cell the  dna is introduced in the cell  um but any remaining dna would be  degraded by  adding benzenes uh so we added benzenes  and um to cells electrocuted at two  energies either energy four or energy  nine  and what you can see is there you get a  recovery  um of cell survival when you add  benzenes  at both energies so there are less cells  at energy  9 but compared without benzenes you do  increase the cell number  at 36 hours post electroporation  and this is just showing the producer  cells remaining  at the time of harvest of nanomedic you  can see that there are only six million  cells remaining if you don't add  benzenes to the electrocreated cells  and you have over three times more cells  when you add benzenes  so what happens to the nanomedic  produced from  from the cells when you add benzene so  the cell  number and viability increased if you  had benzenes and actually so does the  amount of nanomedic produce so if you  add benzenes at energy four  energy nine you get higher yields of  nanomedic being produced from these  producer cells  as indicated by the gfp expression from  the reporter system  so we could increase the amount of  nanomedic by by increasing producer cell  survival  the other thing we found was by  decreasing the amount of  vsvg plasmid which is the surface  envelope used on nanomedic for cell  targeting  we could also increase the um  production of nanomedic and its delivery  into recipient cells  we didn't really look deep into the  mechanism but we believe by reducing  the vsvg amount you're reducing the  amount of  empty particles which might be  um coated with vsvg protein  that might compete with um with  with all in one kind of nanomedic for  delivery so  uh we we got more efficient uh  production  and delivery of nanomedic when we reduce  the amount of vsvg  so after optimizing conditions for  nanomedic  we we then look to produce nanomedic by  flow electroporation  so here you see kind of a range of max  sites  um cuvettes uh from from the left to  right  from the left is the smallest volume you  can work  you know as low as 15 microliters and to  the right you see with the cl2 you can  go as high as 100 mls for  electroporation  we were actually working for the  optimization that um the oc 400 range to  400 microliters of cells in dna  and we were using approximately 60  million cells for electroporation  so after identifying the dna  concentration  and the electroporation energy uh  we could then apply those same  conditions  for the cl2 um so flow electrocution  works by  the cells being in a sample bag and  flowing  three mls at a time into an  electroporation chamber  and and then um sent to a collection bag  so just to give you an example of how  flow electroporation  looks in terms of consistency and  scalability  um if you have 100 mls and this is  looking at k562  cells transfected with gfp plasmid dna  so each 3ml fraction that was  electroporated was  collected and then analyzed for gfp  expression viability  and you can see 30 separate fractions  that were electroporated  had very similar gfp expression  and cell viability and when you compare  that to  um the small scale electroporation with  40 million cells  you can see that the expression  viability were also similar so  going from 40 million cells to 6 billion  cells with the  cl2 gave very consistent and  reproducible results  so in terms of our system how  the how we worked the um the flow of the  experiment was  we um scaled up production of  our um suspension hex cells  in large erlenmeyer flasks and then  we performed flow electroporation of 1.2  billion cells we treated with benzenes  because we found that it helped survive  all the producer cells and then  we we cultured the cells for 36 to 48  hours  in the presence of a dimerization ligand  and then we harvested  nanomedic and we analyzed the amount of  cas9 being incorporated  so in this um  slide what you can see is we compared  production of nanomedic  using 48 10 centimeter plates  um compared with suspension  culture so each one yielded 480 mls of  of culture and what we did was we  we concentrated nanomedic and then we we  lysed the particles  and performed an in vitro assay to  quantify the amount of  active rnp being released  from these cells using both systems and  um  once the rnp is released in in vitro  we add a pcr dna template  which would be cleaved in the presence  of active  cas9 rnp so you should see two cleavage  bands so  on the right here you see a standard  recombinant casino  protein and if you increase the amount  of cas9 protein you increase the amount  of cleavage bends  so we would just compare the amount of  cas9 contained in these  nanometer particles with the standard  curve to quantify the amount of  of cas9 being released  and what we found was that  the adherent system with 480mls release  roughly 27 nanograms per ml of  active rnp complex if we compare that  with kind of a small scale run with 60  million cells and with 1.9 billion cells  there was a reduction of about 34  percent  of the active cas9 being released  however  we see consistency between the small  scale and large scale runs  um for nanomedic production and  considering that we  changed the cell type and we also  changed the media composition completely  and the delivery method um we felt that  the suspension was  easier and more manageable if we wanted  to scale up further  and not only that we were able to um  obtain xeno-free conditions using a  chemically defined media  meaning that there could be potential  clinical application  for the system so  in summary we developed a xeno-free  nanomedic production system by  electroporation  that could be scalable from 60 million  cells to 1.2 billion cells  we found that it technically there it  was easier to handle  and we feel there's promise for  industrial applications  benzene's treatment we found after  electroporation  did enhance cell viability and we were  able to improve nanometric yields  so the energy used for electroporation  if you increase the energy you increase  the amount of nanometer particles  produced  and we did feel at the end that  electroporation was an efficient method  for producing nanomedic  future directions of this project we we  think that the cell targeting can be  changed of nanomedic by changing the  the viral envelope or or maybe adding a  single chain antibody um  the other thing too is that you're not  limited to just cast 9 delivery  you can incorporate any protein of  interest by  fusing a dimerization domain to it i  didn't show it in this presentation but  we were able to efficiently package  luciferous protein  as well into nanomedic and we could  deliver that in in vivo  as well um one could add  a viral antigen and potentially use  nanomedic for vaccine production  um there are certain aspects of the  downstream optimization which are  further needed such as  affinity chromatography purification  which would  increase the purity of nanomedic and and  maybe allow for more efficient delivery  so there are a lot of acknowledgements a  lot of people contributed to this study  uh akitsuholta who was my mentor  and who really helped to um give ideas  for the project  i i'd like to thank him as well as  noriko sasukawa  mandi lang and yuya okazaki who  really helped push the revision of the  experiments i had a lot of help from  summer interns who motivated me to work  harder and and i really appreciate their  support  as well as a collaboration with takeda  and for the t cyber project yukimasa  makita and hiroyuki  hozumi and naoto inukai were responsible  for the in vivo studies  uh june komano gave a lot of ideas for  when i first started the nano medic  production  and um i and other researchers like  takeshi nota who helped with electron  microscopy and yuko fujita  so um with that i'd like to thank you  and i and i'm welcome to take any  questions or comments  um on the right i also want to point out  that we have a research spotlight on  this study  that is downloadable from from a link  that you can find  um and there's a link to the  manuscript itself so again thank you for  your time and  i'm i welcome any questions or comments  thank you  great thank you peter for that  informative presentation  so at this time we will open it up and  use the remaining portion of the session  to ask peter any questions the viewers  might have so if you have a question  you'd like to ask it please do so now  just as a reminder you can use the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen so  let's go ahead and get started so first  question that has come in peter is how  does the neto medic  transient protein delivery system  compare with other systems that are  currently available  well i mean if you compare the the  nanomedic system  with other transient systems i i did try  other published reports like um  of uh the nanoblade system where you  would fuse  cast nine with gag but there are a lot  of limitations of that system because um  you you have to cleave cast nine  uh using a a gag protease to liberate  the protein and that sometimes cuts cas9  itself so  i think with the other systems i i i  didn't find that the guide rna packaging  too was  efficient so at least with other  published reports it  i i was able um i wasn't able to find  both being incorporating the particles  efficiently okay great  um next question how does neto medic  compare with aav delivery  um i think it's it's kind of difficult  to compare both  uh nanomedics a protein-based delivery  system and  an aev just needs a single dna vector to  express a large amount  of cas9 we did some preliminary studies  where we found the kinetics at least of  nanomedic  were faster but but overall i i don't  think we  we fully optimized the um  the conditions for kind of a fair  comparison so  yeah i it's it's if we need to  develop it further okay great  next question is are the tropisms  similar to lentiviral vsvg  and do you need to use immune suppressor  for in vivo use  the tropism is exactly the same because  this particle uses