e small scale on the oc  400 within  two months the only limitation i really  had was was i didn't have too much  uh incubator space where with enough  shakers to do more at one time  but i i was able to optimize and scale  up within i would say  two months up to the cl2 so it was  pretty fast for the optimization  great um looks like next question what  are some other technical challenges in  producing nanomedic  i guess the technical challenges i found  were were the purity of the particles  because  when you concentrate them it's not just  nanomedic but your  your concentrating exosomes or or  potentially some other cell debris so i  i think purification by  affinity chromatography would be pretty  important to remove  any cellular debris or exosomes from the  nanomedic  preparation and also maybe any residual  dna that might be left over  so and also i guess for nanometer  another technical issue could be what  the more you scale up um it was just  more of a  of the culture conditions for a large  cell number which which we didn't  have the capacity for in an academic lab  at the time so  maybe those kind of technical issues  came up  during the optimization  makes sense okay next uh and looks like  final question  is there a risk of other cellular  components being accidentally  incorporated inside of an animatic  particle during production which  potentially may affect the target cell  so we did we did do a proteomics  analysis of the nanometic  particles we we did find some kind of  cellular host proteins but considering  that their  their proteins being incorporated i  think the delivery of those proteins  would be pretty  transient and not in the high  concentrations i  i don't um i don't imagine that there  would be any kind of  toxic issues with that  okay um actually another question did  come in have you looked at the content  of the nanomedic and it's yes  what were the what were were there other  proteins sorry peter  oh sorry so i i guess similar to 